  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe propose a two-stage optimization approach combining convex relaxation with an adaptive projected gradient method. The key idea is to first solve a relaxed convex problem to find a good initial solution, then refine it using a gradient-based method with dynamic step sizes to handle the non-convexity. This approach aims to escape local minima by leveraging the geometric properties of the feasible region and efficiently handling the max-over-shifts objective.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Let h \u2208 \u211d^{n_points} represent the discretized function, with h_i \u2208 [0, 1] for all i.\n- Enforce the constraint \u2211h_i dx = 1, where dx = 2 / n_points.\n- Define the correlation at shift k as (h * (1 - h))[k], where * denotes convolution.\n- The objective is C\u2085 = max_{k} (h * (1 - h))[k] * dx.\n\nWe introduce a surrogate function to handle the max-over-shifts by solving:\nminimize_{h} \u03b1 \u2211_{k} ((h * (1 - h))[k] * dx)^2 + (1 - \u03b1) max_{k} (h * (1 - h))[k] * dx\nsubject to \u2211h_i = n_points / 2, h_i \u2208 [0, 1], where \u03b1 \u2208 (0, 1) balances the two terms.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nA. Initialization\n1. Read initial_h_values if provided.\n2. Set n_points to 200 for detailed resolution.\n3. If no parent is available, initialize h randomly with \u2211h = n_points / 2.\n\nB. Convex relaxation stage\n1. Formulate the problem with CVXPY, minimizing the surrogate function.\n2. Use SCA (Successive Convex Approximation) to iteratively refine the solution.\n3. Project h to [0, 1] and enforce \u2211h = n_points / 2 after each iteration.\n\nC. Refinement stage\n1. Switch to a gradient-based method with adaptive learning rate.\n2. Compute gradients using FFT-based convolution for efficiency.\n3. Adjust the learning rate based on progress: \u03b7 = \u03b7_0 / (1 + k^\u03b2), where k is the step count and \u03b2 controls decay.\n4. Apply trust region constraints to gradient updates to maintain feasibility.\n\nD. Termination and validation\n1. Monitor objective improvement; terminate when <1e-6 change over 100 steps.\n2. Validate final h against constraints and compute C\u2085 using original metric.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Default: n_points=200, \u03b7_0=0.01, \u03b2=0.9.\n- Variations: n_points=150 (faster), n_points=250 (higher precision).\n- Alternative initializations: random within [0,1] with exact integral.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Each CVXPY iteration: ~30s, gradient steps: ~5s each.\n- Budget allocation: 30s convex stage, 500 gradient steps.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Ensure all h_i \u2208 [0,1], and \u2211h_i = n_points / 2.\n- Verify C\u2085 computation matches original correlate-based method.\n- Return best h, C\u2085, and n_points.",
